\begin{example} %%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%% EXAMPLE BOX
Combine the following molecules to produce a nucleotide:
\setchemfig{atom sep=2.5 em,scale=.65}\begin{center}\begin{tikzpicture}
\node (a) at (0,0) {\chemfig{{P(-[:-90,0.8]OH)(-[:90,0.8]OH)(=[:180,0.8]O)(-[:0,0.8]OH)}}};
\node (b) at (2,0)  {    \chemfig{!{thymine}}    };
\node (c) at (5,0)  {\chemfig{!{ribose} } };
\end{tikzpicture}\end{center}
\textlcsc{ \textcolor{dgreen}{\Large \textbf{Solution}} }\\
A nucleotide is the result of combining a sugar--either ribose or deoxyribose--and a base with phosphate. The sugar given is ribose and the base is thymine, so the nucleotide will be part of RNA:
\begin{center}\begin{tikzpicture}
\node (a) at (0,0)  {\chemfig{!{thyminephosphate} } };
\node (a.south east ) [xshift=0cm,yshift=-2.5cm]{\small \textcolor{blue}{  Thymidine-5'-monophosphate   }};
\end{tikzpicture}\end{center}
